# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Every value here is safe to expose publicly.
#
# Note what is not here: nothing about the wallet. There is no app ID, no API
# key and no account with anybody — the wallet is an extension the user has
# already installed, and the Stellar Wallets Kit talks to it in the browser.
# Everything below is about the contribution, not about signing it.
#
# In fact the app runs with no .env.local at all. Every variable here has a
# working default.

# Where contributions go. Defaults to a testnet G-address that already holds a
# USDC trustline, as the interim target. Replace with the pool contract's
# C-address when it is available — no code change is needed, because the only
# behavioural difference is that a contract recipient needs no trustline, and
# that is detected from the address prefix.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POOL_ADDRESS=

# The contributed asset. Defaults to Circle's testnet USDC, whose Stellar Asset
# Contract is already deployed.
#
# These exist as a fallback: if Circle's faucet is flaky, point them at a demo
# asset from a throwaway issuer instead. The SAC is generated per-asset by the
# protocol, so everything downstream is identical — except that a fresh
# issuer's SAC has to be deployed first with createStellarAssetContract.
# scripts/verify-contribution.mts does exactly this.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_CODE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_ISSUER=

# Stellar network settings are deliberately absent. The passphrase, Horizon URL,
# Soroban RPC URL and Friendbot URL are hardcoded to testnet in
# lib/stellar/network.ts so that no deployment can be pointed at mainnet.
#
# Your wallet extension has its own idea of which network it is on, and that
# one is not ours to set. It is checked at connect time instead, and a wallet
# on Public is refused there — see lib/signing/wallets-kit.ts.
